{
  "description": "issue #679 (SP7) + issue #847: pins `clear_screen` when MORE THAN ONE turtle is addressed — the case SP5's single-turtle clear-screen-homes-addressed-turtle left open. `clear_screen` is deliberately NOT a per-turtle command (multiplying it would emit N `clear` events for one canvas clear), so under `tell [ :a :b ]` it emits exactly ONE `clear`, and homes ONLY that current turtle. Since issue #847 that homing is observable: `move` ([0 10] -> [0 0], reporting the pre-reset heading 45) and `turn` (45 -> 0) are emitted before the `clear`, with NO `draw-segment` — the same instruction goes on to clear the drawing, so no line is drawn back to the origin. All three events are stamped with the homed turtle's `turtle_id` (1), so a per-turtle reducer homes exactly :a; the trailing `each` shows :a back at [0 0] heading 0 while :b is still at [0 10] heading 45. \"Current\" is whatever `who` reports — i.e. the FIRST turtle of the addressed set — so this is order-dependent: `tell [ :b :a ]` stamps the homing with :b's id and homes :b instead. A program that wants every addressed turtle homed writes `each [ clear_screen ]`, which emits one `clear` per turtle and homes them all. This is a defensible reading of an ambiguous spec — :111 lists `clear_screen`/`clean` as affecting \"the shared drawing surface\" separately from the per-turtle bullets at :107-110, while :113 says a turtle command applies once per addressed turtle — and it keeps multi-turtle addressing behaviour unchanged (the single-turtle Turtle & Rendering stream does gain the issue #847 homing events, but *which* turtle is homed does not change). The fixture locks the decision so it stays conscious; issue #738 tracks homing every addressed turtle and is deliberately NOT implemented here. Every change of the addressed set — here from `each`/`tell` — also emits the addressing `primitive` event carrying `addressing: { addressed_turtle_ids, current_turtle_id }`, so a consumer can identify the addressed turtle set (issue #766, spec/rendering.md:193); `ask`/`each` emit one again when the previous set is restored, on every exit path.",
